RATIONALE: Two REDs are unrebutted by evidence in the packet, and the prior-art search was not completed — per committee rules, this bars GREEN. More substantively, the evidence picture is clear across all five reviewers:

(1) The universal claim is dead. All reviewers agree; L0's own RESULTS.md commits to "PARTIAL → mostly NEGATIVE" and "claim's universal phrasing FALSIFIED." This is settled.

(2) The conditional survivor is triply undermined. The systems_reviewer found that TensorRT-LLM already ships configurable priority-based LRU with explicit token-range retention policies — the proposed mechanism is not a design contribution but a configuration of an existing system. The product_realist demonstrated that the win regime (cap<50% of hot working set) is operationally irrelevant: no production fleet intentionally provisions this far below the hot set, and the alternative of simply provisioning adequate cache is cheaper and safer. The theory_skeptic showed the win is predicted by textbook 2-class priority cache analysis and may collapse to trivial shared-prefix pinning once properly ablated.

(3) The three YELLOWs do not rescue the claim. The evaluation_prosecutor's YELLOW asks for GPU validation — but a GPU pass under the current claim would reproduce the same crossover on more expensive hardware without addressing the novelty gap (TensorRT-LLM) or the operational irrelevance. The novelty_killer's YELLOW is explicitly precautionary (egress blocked, cannot verify or refute collisions) — the systems_reviewer's independently verified TensorRT-LLM finding resolves the uncertainty in the unfavorable direction. The theory_skeptic's YELLOW credits honest reporting but identifies confounds (single shared_prefix_frac, trace-model dependence, n=3) that weaken even the conditional signal.

(4) What is salvageable. The L0 produced a genuinely useful NEGATIVE finding: the crossover mechanism (conversational scratch has real temporal locality, so 2-class "evict scratch first" backfires with adequate capacity) is well-characterized and honestly reported. This insight could seed a NEW claim about adaptive multi-class policies — but that is a different claim requiring a fresh L0, not a GPU validation of the current one.

I am resolving the 3Y/2R split by evidence weight, not vote count: the two REDs cite concrete, verified evidence (shipping system overlap, operationally irrelevant regime) that the three YELLOWs do not rebut. A YELLOW consensus would imply the claim is conditionally advanceable; the evidence says it is not advanceable under its current formulation. RED is the honest call.

